# Product fields

This maintained reference follows section 4 of Product Documentation SOP, design baseline 0.2. The [Chinese export](产品字段规范.md) and its [provenance](产品字段规范.provenance.json) retain the source wording, source and export hashes, selected section, and references omitted from the bundle. Follow the [common contract](共同约定.md) and the policy supplement (`.formsy-sop/skills/formsy-development-kit/modules/formsy-sop-workflow/references/sop-rules-addendum-20261016/applicability-and-version.md`) for current applicability.

## 4. Product document field standards

Use these blank templates to record the project's actual objects and evidence. Leave missing required fields visible as gaps and explain any finding of non-applicability. A simple feature may use a combined presentation that still covers each relevant check object.

### 4.1 User, problem, and evidence card (PC01/PC04, PM01/PM03)

| Field | Record |
| --- | --- |
| User and problem IDs | U-ID and P-ID |
| Actual or proposed users | Who uses the product, who is affected, and whether the buyer is a different person |
| Scenario and trigger | Conditions that give rise to the task |
| Task and current approach | Current sequence of actions. Distinguish a known absence of a workaround from an unknown approach. |
| Specific difficulty and consequence | Where the task stops, incurs effort or cost, or fails to reach the goal |
| Support and counterexamples | Original user wording, source versions, actual observations, and limits |
| Strength of evidence | Requirement of this user only, applicable observation, assumption, or unknown. Limit the scope of the claim to the evidence. |
| Expected value | User outcome. Use a separate C-ID for claims about market demand, willingness to pay, or efficiency. |
| Material assumptions and next action | Impact, verification method, owner, and closure condition |

The role, task, difficulty, and intended outcome must fit together, with a reviewable basis for the evidence level. Keep material unverified assumptions visible. For a personal tool, explicit requirements from its user can establish the design basis. Scope any broader research to the claim that needs it.

### 4.2 Goals and measurement criteria (PC02/PC11, PM01/PM08)

| Goal ID | Observable user outcome | Linked problem | Decision criterion and unit | Source of the quantitative or qualitative criterion | Environment, scale, and time | Measurement or observation method | Design state and actual result |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| G-ID | What the user can do or obtain after success | P/U-ID | Assessable condition, pending decision if unknown | Decision or baseline location | Applicable boundaries | Collection and comparison method | Design pending confirmation and execution status |

Define what to observe and how to judge each commitment such as speed, reliability, usability, or clarity. For quantitative commitments, record the source, unit, denominator, and measurement definition. Leave unsupported numeric targets, such as milliseconds, satisfaction, or success rates, pending.

### 4.3 Requirements, scope, and priority (PC03/PC04, PM02/PM03)

| Requirement ID | Formal wording or candidate content | Source version and decision | Goal or problem | Category | Priority and rationale | Release scope and version | Acceptance ID | Open issue and impact |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| R-ID | Preserve the original wording and mark candidates explicitly | S/D-ID | G/P-ID | Functional, quality, or delivery | Must, Should, or Could | First release, later release, or explicitly out of scope | A-ID | Open question and any limit on progression |

| Scope tradeoff ID | Required task outcome | Resource bounds and source | Items that can be reduced | Explicit exclusions or deferred items | User impact after reduction | Decision and version |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| SC-ID | Journey outcome | Time, cost, and people. Keep unknowns visible. | Should or Could items and rationale | Excluded capabilities and rationale | Outcomes that remain possible and those affected | D-ID or pending decision |

Account for every required task in its journey and give each scope tradeoff a source. Record the available budget and the work estimate separately. A proposed change to a Must item needs an explicit decision and impact review. RS03 is a method reference for scoping. Establish this project's deadlines and requirement authorization from effective project sources.

### 4.4 Complete journey template (PC05/PC06/PC09, PM04)

| Journey identity field | Record |
| --- | --- |
| Journey ID, name, and user | J-ID, user task, and U-ID |
| Linked goals and requirements | G-ID and R-ID |
| Trigger, entry, and prerequisites | Page, window, or external entry, with existing data and permissions |
| Start and outcome | Initial business state and an outcome the user can recognize |
| Steps outside the application and dependencies | Steps before and after the product, their owners, and which steps the project implements. Record an established absence where applicable. |
| Scope and version | First-release steps, deferred steps, and effective stage version |

| Step or path ID | Prior state | Page or entry | User action | Visible system feedback | Business state or data change | Next step or exit | Feature or rule | Acceptance and evidence plan |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| J-01 normal path | Initial state | UI-ID | Specific action and target object | What the user sees | Before and after | J-02 | F/B-ID | A-ID × platform |
| J-02 branch | Trigger condition | UI-ID | Applicable choice, cancellation, or retry | Message and available next steps | Preserved, reversed, or partially complete | Recovery or termination point | F/B/X-ID | A-ID × platform |

Review applicable first use, normal use, reopening, configuration, exit, cancellation, and recovery. Mark the product boundary for external steps and implement only the steps in scope. RS01 is a method reference for a complete problem description with a bounded increment.

The steps must form a continuous path from the entry to the outcome. Each destination must exist, every material branch must state its result and next action, and the visible feedback must match the business outcome. Give each Must task a complete journey.

